## 2. Fast-Forward Merge

I switched to the `master` branch:

```bash
git checkout master
```

Then merged `feature-login`:

```bash
git merge feature-login
```

Git returned:

```text
Updating a668e00..c61691c
Fast-forward
 hello.txt | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 hello.txt
```

### Observation

This was a **fast-forward merge** because `master` had not moved forward after `feature-login` was created.

Git simply moved the `master` branch pointer to the latest commit of `feature-login`.

### What Is a Fast-Forward Merge?

A fast-forward merge happens when the target branch has no new commits of its own.

Git can simply move the branch pointer forward without creating a separate merge commit.

### Visual Representation

```text
Before:

master
  ↓
  A
   \
    B → C → D
             ↑
        feature-login


After:

master
        ↓
A → B → C → D
             ↑
        feature-login
```

