# Merge vs Rebase vs Squash vs Stash vs Cherry-Pick

| Operation            | Purpose                                | Main Result                        |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `git merge`          | Combines two branch histories          | May create a merge commit          |
| `git rebase`         | Replays commits onto another base      | More linear history                |
| `git merge --squash` | Combines changes from multiple commits | One new commit after manual commit |
| `git stash`          | Temporarily stores uncommitted work    | Clean working directory            |
| `git cherry-pick`    | Applies one specific commit            | Creates a new commit               |

---

# Quick Interview Comparison

| Scenario                            | Command                           |
| ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Combine complete feature branch     | `git merge feature-name`          |
| Keep history linear                 | `git rebase master`               |
| Combine many small feature commits  | `git merge --squash feature-name` |
| Temporarily save unfinished work    | `git stash`                       |
| Restore latest stash                | `git stash pop`                   |
| Restore a specific stash            | `git stash pop stash@{0}`         |
| Apply one specific commit           | `git cherry-pick <commit>`        |
| Continue after cherry-pick conflict | `git cherry-pick --continue`      |
| Cancel cherry-pick                  | `git cherry-pick --abort`         |
| View branch history                 | `git log --oneline --graph --all` |

