# Reflection

## Fast-Forward Merge

A fast-forward merge moves the target branch pointer forward because there are no independent commits on the target branch.

```text
A → B → C
         ↑
     master
```

No additional merge commit is required.

---

## Merge Commit

A merge commit combines two diverged histories into one.

```text
      B---C
     /     \
A---D-------M
```

`M` represents the merge commit.

---

## Rebase

Rebase replays commits on top of another base and produces a more linear history.

```text
Before:

A---B---C
     \
      D---E


After rebase:

A---B---C---D'---E'
```

---

## Squash

Squash combines multiple commits' changes into one new commit on the target branch.

```text
Feature branch:

A → B → C

        ↓ squash

Master:

A → S
```

Where `S` contains the combined changes from `B` and `C`.

---

## Stash

Stash temporarily stores unfinished work so I can switch branches without committing incomplete changes.

```bash
git stash
git checkout another-branch
```

Later:

```bash
git stash pop
```

---

## Cherry-Pick

Cherry-pick lets me apply one particular commit without merging the entire branch.

```bash
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
```

---

# Practical Git Workflow I Learned

## 1. If I Need to Combine a Feature Branch

```bash
git checkout master
git merge feature-name
```

---

## 2. If I Need the Latest Master Changes on My Feature Branch

```bash
git checkout feature-name
git rebase master
```

---

## 3. If I Have Unfinished Work and Need to Switch Branches

```bash
git stash
git checkout another-branch
```

Later:

```bash
git stash pop
```

---

## 4. If I Need Only One Commit from Another Branch

```bash
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
```

If there is a conflict:

```bash
# Resolve the conflicted file
git add <file>

# Continue the cherry-pick
git cherry-pick --continue
```

